{"kind":"report","text":"The landed Page follows the v2 visual direction, but it does not yet provide the prototype's complete content-led workflow. The significant deviations below are discussion items, not prioritized implementation requests. The separate UX planner owns that iteration; they do not change the current B8/reliability ordering.\n\nEvidence boundary\nCurrent main is 38b0b2f2bdb7af6f59076ac3b423741555a7b437, tree 856fddf7f8c2f39dc60fd011bedcdb9a4f600e79. All Page paths, built assets and docs/page.md match independently reviewed C1 candidate b733d663 exactly. I visually inspected all six sprint/demo pictures, five selected v2 reference screenshots, and the later local-label and creation pictures. Relevant Page presentation/action/data spans and reference HTML workflow functions were read; this is not a full Source audit.\n\nThe sprint's room.png and six demo captures are recorded rehearsal evidence at efb5c56d9598ef14831287d9e9d31f06f0592554, with actual native Worker answers and labelled Git-host/scheduler stand-ins. Their Page assets blob is 271506060d614f16f5ffe29c898faa066ee7e8e0. They are not screenshots of current b733/38b0, a new hosted run, or later claim/Join guards. The sprint caption correctly states that distinction. Exact inspected image/source SHA-256 values are in /tmp/artroom-design-comparison-evidence.json.\n\nSignificant deviations for discussion\n\n• Task hierarchy remains weaker than the design. The reference places Create issue in the list toolbar and a useful next action beside the detail title. The sprint picture puts Create issue beneath Inspect room record; detail actions and Comment are collapsed disclosure rows after the content and inspection. Current main still constructs every ordinary action as details and appends the panel after the screen. Rules likewise begins with inspection before its editing form. Impact: useful actions are less discoverable and can require opening a disclosure or scrolling, especially on a phone. Discussion: should the UX owner make the primary offered action directly visible in the toolbar/detail header and reserve disclosures for optional fields and technical acts? Preserve the existing permission and unknown-submit fences.\n\n• The integrated authoring/correction path is incomplete. The reference offers an inline file diff, Propose change with title/path/content, Fix path at the error, and Edit page from reading content. Current main displays authenticated literal source in a disclosure; invalid-path pictures correctly suppress merge/review but offer no ordinary Fix path. The directory's Propose change signs open-pr, while propose-file remains a separate technical action under Inspect. Its actual declared fields include base, content, digest, path and size; the generic form does not compose those from a plain editor. Pages opens the separate latest Site rather than the prototype's editable Page list. Impact: an ordinary person cannot follow the reference's edit → correct → inspect path without technical inputs or the CLI. This is a real native integration gap, not missing sample data. Discussion: what minimum authoring/correction journey should the UX owner specify after the on-path branch workflow, and who owns the native-to-Page adapter? No unlanded B8 compatibility or aa687 work is credited here.\n\n• Rendered preview and merged-page identity are deliberately qualified. The reference's Preview/Open page preserves and renders the selected version. Current Page's Preview source is exact authenticated literal text, while Open latest page/Site HEAD can show later content; no inline diff or rendered Markdown preview is claimed. Impact: reading the published page is not the same operation as inspecting the reviewed version, and the Site surface has repository/branch/commit chrome rather than the prototype's integrated reading/editing controls. This is explicitly accepted by planner 5825722071aaa85ecb46c1d4fdba43ae58a6d59c. A1 selected publication (6ec330fda2e3337b6626683bbe5c6ee024d33e38) owns the immutable-selector follow-up; A2 owns Markdown completeness. Discussion: once A1 is actually landed, should UX connect the merge's exact commit first, or also commission the rendered preview/reading-to-edit path? Keep the current latest label until then.\n\n• Rules exposes more native configuration than the reference. The prototype gives human choices such as No review/1 rules controller. The retained real controller screen uses numeric approvals per actual extent, then Default approvals and Allow author's controller review; advanced configuration is retained separately. Impact: counts are editable, but understanding who counts and the relationship between default and extent counts is harder. The difference also preserves real arbitrary extents/roles/checks/labels/flags rather than substituting the prototype's fixed sample categories. Discussion: should the UX owner define a human role/count presentation over the same full native rule payload, keeping advanced values intact and the existing before/after confirmation? This is a presentation/clarity question, not authority to reduce policy semantics.\n\nQualifications that should not be mistaken for regressions\nThe rail, light/dark palette, unboxed lists, readable typography, subject title and one main current condition substantially match the chosen direction. A different number of issues, the closed rehearsal issue, absent invented names/photos, and no fabricated checks are not design failures.\n\nThe old room.png shows a generated native name. Current 59a6744c5a97c3038751e99625ebb76b3b9e5f7b work supports a room-bound local label beside that name, and its separate narrow picture was inspected. A recorded native typed room name is deliberately deferred after the 14th. Current Create room remains name-only and eligibility-controlled; its later picture was inspected. No cosmetic creation or native-name substitution should be introduced to resemble the local-fixture prototype.\n\nJoin recovery and accepted-comment draft retirement remain explicit C1 follow-ups, including the boundary set by 5ba541ce125c378b358cc066ed965cbf2c577f4f. They are workflow correctness duties, not resolved by this visual comparison. Native membership/authorization, claim recovery, physical devices/assistive technology and live-provider acceptance do not inherit the prototype's sample interaction results.\n\nNo application source, browser/provider state, tests, gates, artifacts or governance actions were changed or run. The outputs are scratch discussion evidence only."}